A||||10||ChadMan||NAPA EGR valve = crap???  What would you do?||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||02-22-2001||05:38 PM||||I stopped by NAPA on the way home from work and picked up an EGR valve for my 88 Coupe (see "The Case of the Stumbling Duke").  The counter guy said the one specifically made for my car was discontinued, but they listed a replacement model that comes with a bunch of different washers that fit in the hole where the plunger is.  I'm supposed to find the stamping number on my original EGR valve and use a table they gave me to pick the correct orifice washer.<P>WTF?  If the size of the orifice is that crucial, shouldn't I worry about leakage around the washer?<P>If you were me, would you just take this EGR valve back to NAPA, order the correct AC/Delco part from the Fiero Store, and wait a week?  Or would you go ahead and install the NAPA part?<P>Thanks for any and all replies.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||02-22-2001||05:44 PM||||I'd get the correct part.  The replacement may work ok, and I've never been a fan of EGR in the first place, but I'd want my car to have the correct parts.  Except, of course, for select modifications I've made myself.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||13
Z||000002||SteveJ||02-22-2001||06:39 PM||||I'd shop another chain and see what they have before I returned this one. Different chain, different suppliers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||02-22-2001||07:29 PM||||Not a big deal. I bought an EGR valve for my 88 coupe from Autozone. It also came with the bag of washers. There should be a number engraved on the top of the old EGR valve. Match that number to the one on the info sheet that came with the EGR valve. Select the appropriate washer and stake it into place on the new valve. <BR>The valves with the washers are very common. <BR>BTW... I replaced the valve because it flunked smog. Passed with flying colors, afterward. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||02-22-2001||08:03 PM||||I bought a NAPA EGR valve for my GT. The original equipment one broke apart. The NAPA one worked fine.  I just matched up the washer required and put it in.  The car ran fine with it.  I ordered the GM replacement and when that came in and when I found the time (I drove to the FOCOA national show in St Louis), I put the GM one in and had a spare EGR valve.  It was one of the easier repairs I ever did on the car. <BR>Almost 1000 posts for Old Lar.||||reg||10
Z||000005||theogre||02-22-2001||08:06 PM||||No. I'm not a big fan of the "universal" EGR units.<P>Yes. It does mater how that washer is intalled. read the emissions article in my cave. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A> I usually paint the side of the washer facing the EGR with a paint on type "sensor safe" sealer for a little more insurance. You are suposed to stake it in at least 3 places. DO NOT use RTV or any other silicon sealer. If any boogers break off they could plug the new valve.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||02-22-2001||09:40 PM||||I bought an autozone one, and it came with one bag of washers, but I think it was supposed to come with two.  I found the correct part number on the table that they gave me, but the washer it listed was not in the bag.  I carefully removed, cleaned up, and reinstalled the washer from the old EGR valve to the new one, have had no driveability problems since.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||02-23-2001||01:42 AM||||Also, on a 13-17 year old car with an unknown history I would be concerned about relying on it having the correct EGR on it. If it's acting up because it's got the wrong EGR... you match the washer to the number on the EGR... ta-da, you have a brand new EGR acting the same way.<P>Considering your next step was going to be removing the head (if I remember right) you should probably go out of your way to make sure you get the right one!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||02-23-2001||08:30 AM||||Thanks, everybody.  I just ordered the AC Delco EGR valve from the Fiero Store and will return the NAPA one today.<P>According to the Haynes manual, the 4-banger takes a "negative backpressure" EGR valve, while the V6 takes a "positive backpressure" EGR valve.  I'm not sure I understand what the difference is, but the NAPA part had "POS" stamped on top -- not encouraging.<P>I'll install it next Saturday...hopefully Saturday night I'll be telling you all how great my Duke runs (turned 119K this morning).||||reg||6
Z||000009||87ECO5||02-23-2001||09:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>... but the NAPA part had "POS" stamped on top -- not encouraging.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"<B>P</B>iece <B>O</B>f <B>S</B>h!t" !?!?!?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||deceler8||02-23-2001||09:10 AM||||Haven't had any problems with my NAPA EGR on my '86 Duke...washer has stayed put.<P>Kind of a tradeoff...the NAPA valve is probably a lot cheaper than an OE valve, but you'll probably feel more confident with an OE valve. The washer thing seems kinda Mickey Mouse, but it seems to work.||||reg||1
